// visio_schema/v1/service/ota/ota.proto
//
// Firmware-update service. Host→device control + bulk data on the
// CONTROL_STREAM_OTA control stream; OtaMessage carries target_device, exactly
// like Command, because a reserved control id is never remapped and so cannot
// itself say which device a frame is for. Device→host progress is published on
// the per-device named channel "/<device>/ota_status" (a dynamic data channel
// advertised via DeviceInfo — NOT a reserved control id), mirroring the Command
// (control id) / CommandResult (named channel) split.
//
// THE NORMATIVE SPEC IS docs/protocol/ota.md. It states the session, what
// bytes_received means, the chunk-size negotiation and its bounds, the board
// mark, and the multi-board package. This file is the message shapes.
//
// ONE PACKAGE, ONE DEVICE. A sender pushes one file to one device and is
// agnostic to what is behind it. A device with limbs attached receives the same
// single file a single-board device does, containing every board's image, and
// updates its own limbs from it — see wire/package.py for that container. So
// nothing here describes a per-board delivery: there is no longer such a thing.
//
// Why its own control stream rather than a Command body: a full image is
// 30–60 MB = tens of thousands of chunks. That is too big for the single-ack
// CommandResult model and too heavy for the small-message COMMAND stream, so
// OTA gets a dedicated stream that carries both control and data frames.
//
// TWO WAYS TO STAGE, chosen by the device's own config, and the wire does not
// change between them. `file` is the shipped ego path, described below.
// `slot` streams each partition straight into the inactive A/B slot as it
// arrives and skips the recovery detour entirely; a sender cannot tell, and
// must not try.
//
// STAGE-THEN-FLASH (`file`). The whole image crosses this stream ONCE, into
// normal mode:
//   * Normal mode receives every OtaChunk and streams it to a staging file on a
//     partition the update never writes (userdata), decrypting and hashing as it
//     goes — so the image is never held whole in RAM, but it IS held whole on
//     disk across the reboot. On OtaCommit it verifies the decrypted image, arms
//     recovery, and reboots.
//   * Recovery reads that staged file and flashes the inactive A/B slot. It
//     speaks NO protocol and touches no network: the receiving half of an
//     earlier design, in which recovery re-received the stream itself, was
//     dropped precisely because a Wi-Fi OTA would then need a network stack (and
//     the decryption key) inside a tiny recovery ramdisk.
//
// A power loss at any point leaves the staged image intact and recovery still
// armed, so the flash simply re-runs; the active slot is never written.
//
// Payload opacity: the bytes are one opaque encrypted image. The host is a BLIND
// RELAY — it pulls the file from the cloud and pushes it unchanged, and only the
// device holds the key. Authenticity rests on that shared key (only a key holder
// can produce a ciphertext that decrypts to a valid image with the declared
// digest), which is why no field here carries a signature.

